# Afterword v2 — Product Requirements Document

**Aurora Labs**
**Last Updated:** March 2026
**Status:** Pre-build

---

## The Problem

You finish a book. You log it on Goodreads. You skim some reviews. You intend to write your own review — you never do. The blank text box is a wall. You don't know where to start, the moment passes, and whatever the book meant to you evaporates into "yeah, it was good."

Afterword v1 tried to solve this with a conversation partner. It didn't work — the founder doesn't use it. The format was wrong. You don't want a chat after finishing a book. You want to be drawn out.

---

## The Insight

The gap between "I have thoughts about this book" and "I can articulate those thoughts" — that gap is the product.

People don't need a conversation partner. They need a set of sharp, book-specific questions that force them to take a position. The review you never write isn't a motivation problem. It's a prompt problem.

---

## One-Liner

**A personal reading shelf where every book carries your take, drawn out by prompts that actually know the book.**

---

## Core Loop

```
Finish a book
    → Search & log it on Afterword
        → 3 book-specific prompts force a position
            → Responses distilled into a shareable card
                → Card lives on your shelf
                    → Share externally if you want
```

---

## What Afterword Replaces

The Goodreads review you never write.

## What Afterword Does NOT Replace

Goodreads logging, book discovery, reading lists, social graph. Afterword is not competing with Goodreads on breadth. It's competing on depth — specifically, the depth of your own thinking about what you read.

---

## Target User (Day One)

The founder. One user. The product must be useful to a single person with no social features, no community, no feed. If it doesn't pass the "would I open this after finishing a book" test, it's wrong.

Broader profile: someone who reads regularly, has opinions about what they read, but never articulates them. They log on Goodreads but the review box stays empty. They'll text a friend about a book but won't write a public take. The friction isn't laziness — it's the blank page.

